.78Z\"></path><path d=\"M47.341,31.501c0,2.086-1.691,3.776-3.778,3.776s-3.782-1.691-3.782-3.776,1.694-3.78,3.782-3.78,3.778,1.691,3.778,3.78Z\"></path><path d=\"M47.341,17.639c0,2.086-1.691,3.78-3.778,3.78s-3.782-1.694-3.782-3.78,1.694-3.78,3.782-3.78,3.778,1.691,3.778,3.78Z\"></path><path d=\"M47.341,3.78c0,2.086-1.691,3.78-3.778,3.78s-3.782-1.694-3.782-3.78,1.694-3.78,3.782-3.78,3.778,1.691,3.778,3.78Z\"></path></svg><div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for dot plot\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul>\n<li>The data for the dot plot are as follows:<br>\n<ul>\n<li>1: 4 dots</li>\n<li>2: 5 dots</li>\n<li>3: 2 dots</li>\n<li>4: 1 dot</li>\n<li>5: 1 dot</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div></figure></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center;\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The dot plot represents a data set of the number of bursts for <math alttext=\"13\"><mn>13</mn>\n</math> eruptions of a steam vent. If an additional eruption with <math alttext=\"11\"><mn>11</mn>\n</math> bursts is added to this data set to create a new data set of <math alttext=\"14\"><mn>14</mn>\n</math> eruptions, which of the following measures will be greater for the new data set than for the original data set?</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-roman;\">\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">The median number of bursts</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">The mean number of bursts</li>\n</ol>","type":"mcq","correct_answer":["C"]},"createDate":1730147357404,"updateDate":1730147357404}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147357404, 1730147357404),
    ($SATQ$e7d9649f$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$01550-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.F.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inference from sample statistics and margin of error $SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">A random sample of 50&nbsp;people from a town with a population of&nbsp;14,878 were asked to name their favorite flavor of ice&nbsp;cream. If 7&nbsp;people in the sample named chocolate as their favorite ice&#8209;cream flavor, about how many people in the town would be expected to name chocolate?</p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<span class=\"align align-44\">350</span>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<span class=\"align align-44\">2,100</span>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<span class=\"align align-44\">7,500</span>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<span class=\"align align-44\">10,500</span>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. Let <span class="italic">x </span>be the number of people in the entire town that would be expected to name chocolate. Since the sample of 50 people was selected at random, it is reasonable to expect that the proportion of people who named chocolate as their favorite ice-cream flavor would be the same for both the sample and the town population. Symbolically, this can be expressed as <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e7d9649f/img_000_427605b2.png" alt="the fraction 7 over 50, end fraction, equals, the fraction x over 14,878"></span>. Using cross multiplication,&nbsp;<span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e7d9649f/img_001_f7822de7.png" alt="7 times 14,878 equals, x times 50"></span>; solving for <span class="italic">x</span> yields 2,083. The choice closest to the value of 2,083 is choice B, 2,100.<p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect and may be the result of errors when setting up the proportion, solving for the unknown, or incorrectly comparing the choices to the number of people expected to name chocolate, 2,083.</p></p>
$SATQ$, false, 2, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"e7d9649f","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"01550-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.F.","skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A random sample of 50&nbsp;people from a town with a population of&nbsp;14,878 were asked to name their favorite flavor of ice&nbsp;cream. If 7&nbsp;people in the sample named chocolate as their favorite ice&#8209;cream flavor, about how many people in the town would be expected to name chocolate?</p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<span class=\"align align-44\">350</span>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<span class=\"align align-44\">2,100</span>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<span class=\"align align-44\">7,500</span>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<span class=\"align align-44\">10,500</span>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. Let <span class=\"italic\">x </span>be the number of people in the entire town that would be expected to name chocolate. Since the sample of 50 people was selected at random, it is reasonable to expect that the proportion of people who named chocolate as their favorite ice-cream flavor would be the same for both the sample and the town population. Symbolically, this can be expressed as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e7d9649f/img_000_427605b2.png\" alt=\"the fraction 7 over 50, end fraction, equals, the fraction x over 14,878\"></span>. Using cross multiplication,&nbsp;<span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e7d9649f/img_001_f7822de7.png\" alt=\"7 times 14,878 equals, x times 50\"></span>; solving for <span class=\"italic\">x</span> yields 2,083. The choice closest to the value of 2,083 is choice B, 2,100.<p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect and may be the result of errors when setting up the proportion, solving for the unknown, or incorrectly comparing the choices to the number of people expected to name chocolate, 2,083.</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":2,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959614,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"e7d9649f","skill_cd":"Q.F.","score_band_range_cd":2,"skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","createDate":1691007959614,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"01550-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"4aed34b8-3b40-48f7-b587-2b080f0ad2d4","difficulty":"E"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"01550-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">A random sample of 50&nbsp;people from a town with a population of&nbsp;14,878 were asked to name their favorite flavor of ice&nbsp;cream. If 7&nbsp;people in the sample named chocolate as their favorite ice&#8209;cream flavor, about how many people in the town would be expected to name chocolate?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<span class=\"align align-44\">350</span>\n"},"b":{"body":"<span class=\"align align-44\">2,100</span>\n"},"c":{"body":"<span class=\"align align-44\">7,500</span>\n"},"d":{"body":"<span class=\"align align-44\">10,500</span>\n"}},"correct_choice":"b","rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. Let <span class=\"italic\">x </span>be the number of people in the entire town that would be expected to name chocolate. Since the sample of 50 people was selected at random, it is reasonable to expect that the proportion of people who named chocolate as their favorite ice-cream flavor would be the same for both the sample and the town population. Symbolically, this can be expressed as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the fraction 7 over 50, end fraction, equals, the fraction x over 14,878\"></span>. Using cross multiplication,&nbsp;<span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"7 times 14,878 equals, x times 50\"></span>; solving for <span class=\"italic\">x</span> yields 2,083. The choice closest to the value of 2,083 is choice B, 2,100.<p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect and may be the result of errors when setting up the proportion, solving for the unknown, or incorrectly comparing the choices to the number of people expected to name chocolate, 2,083.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959614,"updateDate":1691007959614}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959614, 1691007959614),
    ($SATQ$e821a26d$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$10145-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.D.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Two-variable data: Models and scatterplots$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<div class="stimulus_reference " xmlns="http://www.imsglobal.org/xsd/apip/apipv1p0/qtiitem/imsqti_v2p1"><div class="passage "><div class="prose style:1 "><p class="passage_para ">The scatterplot below shows the amount of electric energy generated, in millions of megawatt-hours, by nuclear sources over a 10&#8209;year period.</p></div></div><div class="standalone_image "><div class="image-options-wrapper image-wrap-center 47732 tcp-a6bf33b0-30a4-460d-9570-c8e11a57456b"><img src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e821a26d/img_004_12019106.png" alt="The figure presents a scatterplot in the xy-plane titled &ldquo;Electric Energy Generated by Nuclear Sources.&rdquo; The x-axis is labeled &ldquo;Time,&rdquo; in years, and the numbers zero through 12, in increments of 2, are indicated. The y-axis is labeled &ldquo;Energy,&rdquo; in million megawatt-hours, and the numbers 700 through 820, in increments of 20, are indicated. There are 10 data points scattered in the form of a curve. The first data point begins approximately at the point with coordinates 1 comma 764. The points follow a path varying upward and downward and to the right, until reaching the point with coordinates of approximately 8 comma 806. The points then move downward and to the right, ending at the point with coordinates of approximately 10 comma 768. 
The data represented by the points are as follows. Note that all values are approximate.
Point 1: 1 year, 764 million mega-watt hours. 
Point 2: 2 years, 788 million mega-watt hours.
Point 3: 3 years, 781 million mega-watt hours.
Point 4: 4 years, 785 million mega-watt hours.
Point 5: 5 years, 804 million mega-watt hours.
Point 6: 6 years, 803 million mega-watt hours.
Point 7: 7 years, 796 million mega-watt hours.
Point 8: 8 years, 806 million mega-watt hours.
Point 9: 9 years, 790 million mega-watt hours.
Point 10: 10 years, 768 million mega-watt hours.
